{"name":"Mauve Shadows","hex":"#af8f9c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"mauve","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A rich purple synthetic dye, which faded easily, briefly popular c. 1859‒1873 and now called mauveine.","A pale purple or violet colour, like the colour of the dye after it has faded."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a pale purple colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mauve"},{"term":"shadows","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of shadow"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["third-person singular simple present indicative of shadow"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/shadows"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mauve","blocks":[{"text":"Mauve is a pale purple color named after the mallow flower. The first use of the word mauve as a color was in 1796–1798 according to the Oxford English Dictionary, but its use seems to have been rare before 1859. Another name for the color is mallow, with the first recorded use of mallow as a color name in English in 1611."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Wilde_Malve.JPG/330px-Wilde_Malve.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mauve","wikidata":"Q604079"},{"title":"Shadow","blocks":[{"text":"A shadow is a dark area on a surface where light from a light source is blocked by an object. In contrast, shade occupies the three-dimensional volume behind an object with light in front of it. The cross-section of a shadow is a two-dimensional silhouette, or a reverse projection of the object blocking the light."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/The_shadow_of_a_musician_playing_transverse_flute.jpg/330px-The_shadow_of_a_musician_playing_transverse_flute.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shadow","wikidata":"Q160020"}],"images":[{"srcse